  {
    q: 'What government or PSU jobs are open to BSc biotechnology graduates?',
    a: 'BIBCOL (a Central PSU under the Department of Biotechnology) and HLL Biotech/HLL Lifecare recruit BSc and MSc life-science graduates for vaccine and pharmaceutical production roles, often on contract. CSIR technical-assistant postings and state agriculture or pollution-control-board lab roles are also real, though several expect prior lab experience alongside the BSc.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which MSc specialisation should I choose after BSc biotechnology?',
    a: 'Match it to what you actually enjoyed in your BSc, not to what sounds most impressive. Genetic engineering or genomics suits students who liked cloning and sequencing work; bioinformatics suits those comfortable with data alongside wet-lab work; industrial biotechnology leads into biopharma manufacturing; medical or pharmaceutical biotechnology feeds drug discovery and regulatory-adjacent roles; agricultural or plant biotechnology leads into seed companies and ICAR-linked research. Check each institute’s placement record for your shortlisted specialisation before committing.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the college actually matter for a BSc in biotechnology?',
    a: 'Yes, more than the degree title does. Research-heavy 4-year BS or integrated programmes (IISc, BITS Pilani, IIT Madras-linked tracks) set the placement ceiling, with specialised R&D roles starting around ₹10–12 LPA. Strong-placement affiliated colleges such as St. Xavier’s College Kolkata, St. Joseph’s College, and Loyola College Chennai report roughly 80% placement with recruiters like Biocon and Novartis. Large private-university networks such as Amity Noida, VIT Vellore, and Chandigarh University run big biotechnology departments inside a much larger campus placement system — always ask for the biotechnology-specific placement number, not the university-wide figure.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I do an MBA instead of an MSc after BSc biotechnology?',
    a: 'Only if you genuinely want the business side of the industry — product management, regulatory-affairs management, market access, or healthcare consulting — not just because you are tired of lab work. An MBA in Pharmaceutical Management (or a general MBA with a healthcare specialisation) needs a bachelor’s degree with 50–60% marks plus an entrance score like CAT, MAT, XAT, CMAT, or GMAT. Entry pay commonly runs ₹4–8 LPA, rising toward ₹15–35+ LPA over several years for strong performers — but the MSc route stays the more direct path if research, regulatory science, or lab leadership is the actual goal.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
