      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Eligibility windows and cutoffs change every year, and GAT-B itself replaced the older JNU CEEB exam in recent years — verify the current brochure on the official <a href="https://jam2026.iitb.ac.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">JAM</a>, <a href="https://cuet.nta.nic.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CUET-PG</a>, and <a href="https://rcb.res.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RCB (GAT-B)</a> pages before assuming last year’s pattern still applies.
  </p>

  <p class="note-line">
    The three exams also differ in format, not just eligibility. JAM runs as a roughly 3-hour computer-based test with 60 questions worth 100 marks (MCQs, MSQs, and numerical-answer questions, with negative marking only in one section). GAT-B runs longer — 240 marks across 160 questions, of which you attempt 120 — and charges a non-refundable fee of around ₹1,300 (₹650 for SC/ST/PwD candidates). CUET-PG is the shortest: 75 MCQs in 90 minutes, with +4 for a correct answer and −1 for a wrong one. Budget exam fees and the actual time commitment into your prep plan, not just tuition.
  </p>

